 ],
 "literal": "Him who thinks of me first, I thought of later! / Let my mind sink! Let my tongue be split! / Like a well in a ruined village, let my ears be blocked! / The yak that grazed on fragrant narandhai grass, / having drunk from the fresh mountain pool of blue water lilies, / dwells with its female mate in the cool shade of the nearby takaram tree. / In the northern direction is the sky-touching Himalayas. / In the southern direction, if the clan of Ay were not there, / this wide-spread world would surely tilt and lose its balance!",
 "translation": "I thought of him only later, though he thought of me first! Let my mind sink, let my tongue be split, and let my ears be blocked like a well in a ruined village! In the north stands the sky-touching Himalayas. In the south, if the clan of chieftain Ay did not exist, this wide, sprawling world would surely tilt and lose its balance. (Just as the Himalayas balance the north, Ay's clan balances the south, where yaks graze on fragrant grass, drink from fresh lily pools, and rest with their mates in the cool shade of takaram trees.)",
 "modern": "என்னை முதலில் நினைத்தவனை நான் தாமதமாக நினைத்தேனே! என் நெஞ்சம் அழியட்டும்; என் நாக்கு பிளக்கட்டும்; பாழடைந்த ஊர்க் கிணற்றைப் போல என் காதுகள் தூர்ந்து போகட்டும்! நரந்தை என்னும் நறுமணப் புல்லை மேய்ந்த கவரி மான், குவளை மலர்கள் நிறைந்த குளிர்ந்த சுனை நீரைக் குடித்துவிட்டு, அருகில் உள்ள தகர மரத்தின் குளிர்ந்த நிழலில் தன் பெண் துணையோடு தங்கியிருக்கும். அத்தகைய பொதிகை மலையை உடைய தென்திசையில் ஆய் அண்டிரனின் குடி இல்லையென்றால், வடதிசையில் உள்ள வானளாவிய இமயமலைக்கு இணையாகத் தாங்கிப் பிடிக்க ஆள் இல்லாமல், இந்த அகன்ற உலகம் நிலைதடுமாறிச் சாய்ந்துவிடுமே!",
 "explanation": "The poet Uraiyur Enicceri Mudamociyar sings this poem in praise of the chieftain Ay Andiran. The poet begins with deep self-reproach, wishing that his mind would sink, his tongue be split, and his ears blocked like an abandoned well because he failed to think of his generous patron first. He then describes the beautiful, peaceful southern hills of Ay, where a wild yak grazes on fragrant grass, drinks from a fresh lily pool, and rests with its mate under the cool shade of a takaram tree. The poem culminates in a grand cosmic compliment: while the sky-touching Himalayas anchor the northern direction, it is the noble clan of Ay that balances the south. Without Ay's presence to hold up the southern end, the poet declares that the entire wide world would lose its balance and tilt.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}